 ### [Face Culling](8735-face-culling.md)
-Face Culling is a mesh generation optimization that prevents rendering block faces occluded by adjacent solid voxels, significantly reducing triangle count in homogeneous voxel terrain. It solves the problem of naively rendering all 6 faces of every cube, including those hidden by neighbors, by exposing only faces adjacent to empty space or out-of-bounds. Unlike [Voxel Block Placement with Face Culling](923-voxel-block-placement-with-face-culling.md) which may handle placement logic, or [Face Coverage Mask Culling](3397-face-coverage-mask-culling.md) which uses coverage masks, this method relies on direct neighbor occupancy checks during mesh construction.
+Face Culling is a topological optimization used during voxel mesh generation to exclude geometric primitives that do not contribute to the final image by identifying faces occluded by adjacent solid voxels. It solves the problem of naively rendering all six faces of every solid cube, which results in massive overdraw and poor performance in homogeneous terrain. Unlike view-relative methods like [Voxel Backface Culling and Internal Face Masking](4569-voxel-backface-culling-and-internal-face-masking.md) which depend on camera orientation, Face Culling is static and determined solely by the local occupancy of the voxel grid.
 
 ### [Opacity-based Mesh Culling](8759-opacity-based-mesh-culling.md)
-Opacity-based Mesh Culling is a post-processing optimization technique applied to polygonal meshes extracted from neural radiance fields or volumetric representations. It solves the problem of excessive mesh complexity and storage overhead by discarding geometric primitives that contribute negligible visual information. Unlike sibling methods that rely on spatial occupancy or block visibility [Face Culling and Visibility Optimization](3861-face-culling-and-visibility-optimization.md), this method evaluates the radiometric contribution (opacity) of individual faces or triangles, retaining only those that meet a minimum opacity threshold. This results in a significant reduction in mesh count while preserving visual fidelity.
+Opacity-based Mesh Culling is a post-processing optimization technique used to reduce the geometric complexity of extracted polygonal meshes by discarding faces that contribute negligible visual information. It operates by evaluating the opacity (or density) of mesh faces against a predefined threshold, retaining only those that are sufficiently opaque to impact the final image. This method distinguishes itself from topological culling, which relies on voxel neighbor occupancy, and view-relative culling, which depends on camera orientation, by focusing purely on the material property (opacity) of the geometry itself. It is primarily used to reduce storage costs and improve rasterization performance by decreasing the triangle count, often resulting in a significant reduction (e.g., to 5% of the original count).
 
 ### [From-Region Visibility Preprocessing](9048-from-region-visibility-preprocessing.md)
-From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique that precomputes which geometric primitives are visible from specific regions of a scene to optimize rendering performance. It solves the problem of redundant rendering of occluded geometry by determining visibility sets for spatial regions in advance. It differs from sibling methods like [Face Culling](8735-face-culling.md) which operate on local voxel adjacency, by operating on global or semi-global geometric occlusion relationships.
+From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique that precomputes which geometric primitives are visible from specific regions of the viewing space, allowing for the culling of occluded geometry during rendering. It differs from view-relative culling methods like [Voxel Backface Culling and Internal Face Masking](4569-voxel-backface-culling-and-internal-face-masking.md) by being independent of the camera's instantaneous position, and from topological culling like [Face Culling](8735-face-culling.md) by handling complex, non-voxel-based geometry where neighbor relationships are not sufficient to determine occlusion.
 
 ### Buried Bitmask Optimization
-A bitmask used to track which blocks in a chunk are 'buried' (fully surrounded by opaque blocks), allowing the renderer to skip mesh generation for those blocks [S2, unofficial]. The process resets the buried bitmask for the chunk, creates mapped adjacent chunks for neighbor access, and iterates through all blocks to determine if they are buried via `block->isBuried(...)`. If all blocks in a chunk are buried, the mesh is marked empty and generation is skipped. During mesh generation, any block that is air or has its buried flag set is skipped [S2, unofficial].
